@packages/compiler/src/adapter.sigil import { SigilAgentAdapter }
@packages/compiler/src/evaluation-request.sigil import { SigilAgentEvaluationRequest }
@packages/compiler/src/evaluation-capabilities.sigil import { SigilAgentCapabilityContract }
@packages/compiler/src/adapter-execution-coordinator.sigil import { SigilAgentAdapterExecutionCoordinator }

expand SigilAgentAdapter {
  logic {
    AgentAdapter {
      Supply the target request, compact evaluator retrieval brief, evaluation
      guidance, output contract, workspace root, and requested capabilities to the
      selected adapter implementation.

      At adapter-execution entry, capture the monotonic elapsed-time origin. Before
      invocation, check already-requested cancellation, canonical observability
      binding equality, capability compatibility, implementation input preflight,
      elapsed-time exhaustion, and preventive budget exhaustion in that order; return
      only the first failure and invoke no provider.

      Delegate the final cancellation observation and invocation transition to
      SigilAgentAdapterExecutionCoordinator.

      As part of capability compatibility, translate required restrictions into the
      selected adapter's documented provider configuration. If translation cannot be
      constructed, reject capability-mismatch; construction remains unverified.

      Invalid or incomplete provider-neutral request evidence rejects
      incomplete-evidence without invoking the provider. After validation,
      implementation input preflight serializes and bounds the compact evaluator
      brief, request controls, and restriction configuration. Serialization failure
      or maxInitialRequestChars overflow rejects operational-limit without invoking
      the provider.

      Invoke the provider once through the selected adapter's implementation-owned
      execution mechanism. Consume its declared result inputs according to that
      implementation's ordering and settlement rules.

      Supply a deferred provider-specific execution handle plus invocation,
      result-input, resource-observation, and cleanup hooks to
      SigilAgentAdapterExecutionCoordinator. Map CoordinatorResult or
      CoordinatorFailure to AgentEvaluationResult or AdapterFailure without
      reinterpreting arbitration or cleanup policy.

      Incrementally parse provider framing, bound retained nonessential command
      content, and locate exactly one terminal result.

      Translate provider-specific framing, result locations, telemetry, and command
      observations into AgentEvaluationResult. Do not accept or rewrite candidate
      finding rules or evidence. Validate the envelope through
      SigilAgentEvaluationRequest and return findings as untrusted candidates.

      Preflight preventive-budget exhaustion starts no provider execution. Live
      preventive exhaustion requests an implementation-owned terminal transition and
      cleanup. Return valid post-settlement results intact.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    CapabilityPolicy {
      Require the compatibility result owned by SigilAgentCapabilityContract before
      invocation; a mismatch fails without duplicating its equality algorithm.

      Provider, implementation identity, exact version, model, and observability do
      not participate in capability equality.

      Capability declarations are self-reported compatibility metadata. Explicit
      adapter selection accepts documented implementation risk without adding a
      generic runtime warning to compiler evidence.
    }

    ProviderMapping {
      A provider identity is an opaque nonempty identifier declared by its adapter
      package. Provider-specific commands, events, result locations, and telemetry
      remain private adapter details.

      Initial request overflow rejects as operational-limit before invocation.
      Provider framing overflow rejects as operational-limit and triggers
      implementation-owned bounded cleanup. Final-result size overflow rejects as
      final-result-protocol after execution and declared result-input settlement.
      Candidate findings remain untrusted until compiler rule and evidence
      normalization.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    ProviderNeutralEvaluation {
      Decision: Keep provider implementations behind one public evaluation request
      and result contract. AgentAdapter owns provider mapping and the public
      result/failure surface, and delegates provider-neutral coordination policy to
      SigilAgentAdapterExecutionCoordinator.

      Scope: Governs provider-specific adapter mapping; excludes exact registration,
      coordinator policy, resource declarations, and cleanup actions.

      Trade-offs: Each provider retains version-sensitive protocol mapping.

      Consequences: Changing provider integration does not change compiler diagnostic
      semantics or registry behavior.
    }

    OpaqueProviderIdentitySet {
      Decision: Treat a provider identity as an opaque identifier owned by its
      adapter package and register implementations supplied by the compiler host.

      Scope: Governs registry and evaluator provider identity; excludes adapter
      implementation trust and provider protocol mapping.

      Trade-offs: The semantic namespace stays predictable while adding a provider
      requires an intentional contract update.

      Consequences: Separate packages may add implementations without silently
      extending provider identity.
    }

    DeterministicPreflightFailurePrecedence {
      Decision: Capture the monotonic elapsed-time origin at adapter-execution entry,
      then check cancellation, observability binding, capability compatibility
      including restriction-configuration construction, implementation input
      preflight, elapsed-time exhaustion, and preventive budget exhaustion in that
      order.

      Scope: Governs simultaneous pre-invocation failures only.

      Trade-offs: One stable failure hides later preflight defects until corrected.

      Design issues addressed: Independent checks otherwise expose implementation
      order and produce nondeterministic caller behavior.

      Discarded alternatives: Aggregation was rejected because cancellation should
      stop inspection and identity must precede behavior, transport, time, and
      budget.

      Consequences: Equivalent invalid requests return the same failure.
    }

    TerminalResultOnlyAdapterProgress {
      Decision: Define progress none as absence of a live adapter observation
      channel. Permit optional provider progress observations only inside validated
      terminal result metadata in this compiler version.

      Scope: Governs provider progress; excludes compiler-owned stage lifecycle
      events emitted through CompilationEventWriter.

      Trade-offs: Provider progress is unavailable while evaluation runs, avoiding a
      second public envelope and backpressure protocol.

      Consequences: Every bundled adapter declares progress none; terminal progress,
      command, and usage metadata may still appear in the validated terminal result
      and never alter canonical observability binding.

      Revisit when: A provider-neutral live observation envelope is designed.
    }

    CompleteProviderResultInputValidation {
      Decision: Validate terminal-result framing and size only after provider
      execution settles and all implementation-declared result inputs complete.

      Scope: Governs successful-result eligibility and final-result-protocol failure;
      excludes earlier cancellation, limits, and execution failures.

      Trade-offs: A known oversized terminal candidate may continue until bounded
      implementation settlement, preserving duplicate and trailing-framing detection.

      Discarded alternatives: Accepting or rejecting a terminal candidate before
      declared result-input completion was rejected because later framing can
      invalidate it.

      Consequences: Final-result overflow returns no partial result and rejects only
      after provider and declared result-input settlement.
    }

    ExternalAdapterTrustBoundary {
      Decision: Treat adapter capabilities as self-declared and make explicit adapter
      selection acceptance of documented execution risk.

      Scope: Governs compiler-owned and externally supplied adapters.

      Consequences: Matching metadata is not a verified safety guarantee and generic
      risk warnings do not contaminate compiler evidence.
    }

    SingleAttemptExecution {
      Decision: Invoke one provider execution once per adapter evaluation and leave
      retry to a new caller evaluation.

      Scope: Governs invocation, execution, protocol, and result failures.

      Consequences: Each evaluation has one execution attempt and unambiguous budget
      accounting.
    }
  }

  cases {
    AgentAdapter {
      An incompatible capability declaration, or inability to construct its
      documented provider restriction configuration, fails before invocation.

      When every preflight condition fails, cancellation wins. Removing each earlier
      condition returns binding-mismatch, capability-mismatch, operational-limit,
      elapsed-time, then preventive-budget in order, and no case invokes the
      provider.

      Invalid or incomplete request evidence rejects incomplete-evidence before
      provider invocation. Input serialization failure and maxInitialRequestChars
      overflow reject operational-limit before provider invocation.

      Unequal request and selected-adapter observability rejects as binding-mismatch
      before provider invocation and starts no execution.

      A terminal-only adapter returns one valid final result without live provider
      progress.

      Retained nonessential command content crossing its bound is truncated or
      discarded and does not reject an otherwise valid result.

      Duplicate, missing, malformed, or trailing terminal framing rejects as
      final-result-protocol and returns no result.

      An adapter may treat provider framing inside one open turn as nonterminal
      checkpoints and settle a candidate only at its provider's completion boundary.
      Framing after that boundary rejects as final-result-protocol.

      An oversized final result waits for provider and declared result-input
      settlement, then rejects as final-result-protocol without returning a partial
      result.

      Live preventive-budget exhaustion participates in coordinator arbitration.
      Verified cleanup returns preventive-budget; unverified cleanup returns cleanup
      with recovery evidence; a result settled in an earlier batch remains final.

      Provider-specific hooks produce one coordinator-settled valid result or typed
      AdapterFailure without adapter-local arbitration.
    }
  }
}
